USERADD(8)                   System Administration Commands                   USERADD(8)

NAME
       useradd - create a new user account on the system

SYNOPSIS
       useradd [OPTIONS] USERNAME

DESCRIPTION
       The useradd command creates new user accounts on the system. If no options are 
       specified, the system will use default settings for the user's account.

       By default, useradd creates the user's account with default configurations, 
       but system administrators can customize user creation using a variety of 
       options to set specific properties for the user.

BASIC USAGE
       To create a new user with default settings, use the following command:
       
           useradd <username>

       Example:
       
           useradd john

       This command creates a new user named "john" with default system settings, 
       which include default group assignment, default home directory, and default 
       shell (usually /bin/bash).

OPTIONS
       The following options allow customization of the user's account during creation:

       -g <groupname>
              Specifies the group to which the user will belong. This group must exist 
              on the system before using this option.

              Example:
              
                  useradd -g developers john

              This adds the user "john" to the existing "developers" group.

       -s <shell>
              Specifies the login shell for the new user. The shell must be available 
              on the system (e.g., /bin/bash, /bin/zsh, /bin/sh).

              Example:
              
                  useradd -s /bin/zsh john

              This sets the login shell for the user "john" to Zsh (/bin/zsh).

       -m
              Creates a new home directory for the user in /home/<username>. Without 
              this option, no home directory will be created for the user.

              Example:
              
                  useradd -m john

              This creates a new home directory at /home/john for the user "john".

       -d <home directory path>
              Specifies a custom path for the user's home directory. This option 
              overrides the default home directory location (/home/<username>).

              Example:
              
                  useradd -d /custom/path/john -m john

              This creates a home directory for "john" at /custom/path/john.

       -c <user description>
              Sets a comment (also called GECOS information) for the user. This is 
              typically used to store the user's full name or a short description.

              Example:
              
                  useradd -c "John Doe - Web Developer" john

              This adds the comment "John Doe - Web Developer" to the user "john".

ADVANCED USAGE
       To create a more customized user with a combination of options, use the following 
       format:

           useradd -g <groupname> -s <shell> -c <user description> -m -d <home directory path> <username>

       Example:
       
           useradd -g developers -s /bin/bash -c "John Doe - Senior Developer" -m -d /projects/john john

       This command creates the user "john" with the following properties:
       - Group: "developers"
       - Shell: /bin/bash
       - Description: "John Doe - Senior Developer"
       - Home directory: /projects/john (created if it does not exist)

FILES
       /etc/passwd
              Contains user account information.

       /etc/shadow
              Contains encrypted password information for user accounts.

       /etc/group
              Contains information about groups and their members.

EXIT STATUS
       The useradd command returns 0 if successful. If an error occurs, a non-zero 
       exit status is returned.

